Verdict: PASS. The fix for #1057 does what the task asked. I wrote the verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• What the fix does: when a --bg worker starts in a worktree, its path is now saved on the tracker entry. Before each /spawn, yoyo cleans up worktrees older than an hour (the stale sweep), and that cleanup now skips the worktree of any worker that is still running.
• How the decision is made: it sits in a separate function, worktrees_to_sweep, which takes the list of live worktrees as an argument. It normalizes paths on both sides before comparing, so a trailing slash, a . in the path or a symlinked spelling still match.
• Tests: a table covers every case the task listed, plus a symlink case and an end-to-end case with a real tracker: a running worker's worktree is kept, and it is removed once the worker completes. A dirty worktree is still kept, as covered by the existing tests.
• Nothing left unused: the new field, set_worktree and live_worktrees are all called from real code. Every caller of cleanup_stale_worktrees uses the new signature.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry are updated. The task required a new issue for workers owned by another yoyo process; that issue, #1097, exists and is open.

I didn't re-run the build or tests (the harness had already passed them) or run the binary. I judged from the diff, a search for callers and the #1057 references, and a check of #1097's status. I also didn't repeat the positive control; the claim that it went red comes from the implementer's note in ARCHITECTURE.md.
